@image html tutorials/exahype2/exagrype/illustrations/exagrype_logo.png

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents

ExaGRyPE is a numerical relativity application built on top of the ExaHyPE2 engine.
At its heart, it simulates spacetimes by solving the Einstein field equations
in the CCZ4 formulation under a 3+1 foliation.
The code offers a variety of numerical schemes and ingredients.
For example, we offer a first-order CCZ4 (FO-CCZ4) and a first-/second-order
reconstructed CCZ4 formalism, and for both provide Finite Volume, higher-order
Finite Difference, and Discontinuous Galerkin schemes.
Some of these schemes can then be modified, e.g. by adding Kreiss-Oliger
dissipation or lop-sided advection.
As all of the code is built on top of ExaHyPE, which is a generic PDE solver
engine, we can couple the resulting solution of the field equations with other
solvers on top.

The ExaGRyPE release paper is provided below for interested users. The code is
completely open source and can be found in the Peano repositories.
To install and run the code, start with
\ref tutorials_exahype2_applications_exagrype_configure. It leads on to the
ExaGRyPE tutorial notebooks, which are part of the
@ref page_exahype2_tutorials "ExaHyPE 2 tutorials".
As it is under active development, we cannot offer any support or guarantee
that all variants always work properly.
However, there is an active Slack community around the code, and interested
colleagues are invited to join by dropping us an email.

<div style="background-color: #ccf ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;"> 
  As applications like ExaGRyPE are built on top of ExaHyPE, we decided to embed 
  all application-specific content directly into ExaHyPE, which in turn is now 
  integrated into Peano. Consequently, you get the whole package once you download
  Peano. Another consequence is that a lot of "how to use ExaGRyPE" content is 
  delivered as tutorials that are part of a larger set of tutorials on ExaHyPE and
  Peano. We might refer to generic tutorials in some places, but this is only 
  because the features discussed apply 1:1 to ExaGRyPE, too.
</div>


# Physics notes on the vacuum spacetime

The ExaGRyPE code solves the Einstein equations in the Z4 formulation

@f$
    R_{ab}-\frac{1}{2} g_{ab}R+\nabla_{a} Z_{b}+\nabla_{b} Z_{a}-g_{ab}\nabla^c
    Z_c -\kappa_{1}[n_{a} Z_{b}+n_{b} Z_{a}+\kappa_{2} g_{ab} n_{c}
    Z^{c}]  =  8\pi T_{ab}
@f$

Notably, we transfer them into a first-order formulation following

@f$
  \partial_t Q +\nabla_i F_i(Q) +B_i (Q) \nabla_i Q = S(Q).
@f$

Collecting all derived quantities from the CCZ4 decomposition, the gauge
quantities, and helper variables yields an evolving system of 25 variables

@f$
  Q(t) = 
  \left( \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta,
  \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i \right)(t),
@f$

if the auxiliary variables representing the first-order derivatives

@f$
   A_{i}:=\partial_{i} \alpha, \ B_{k}^{i}:=\partial_{k} \beta^{i}, \ D_{k i
   j}:=\frac{1}{2} \partial_{k} \tilde{\gamma}_{i j}, \
   P_{i}:=\partial_{i} \phi
@f$

are calculated on the fly. In this case, we have the second-order formulation
of the code.
Adding the auxiliary variables to the system and treating them as further
evolving variables makes the code completely first-order, yielding a system of
58 evolved variables with

@f$
  Q(t) =
  \left(
   \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}, \alpha, \beta^i, \phi, \tilde{A}_{ij},K, \Theta,
   \hat{\Gamma}^i, b^i, A_k, B^i_k, D_{kij}, P_k \right).
@f$

The code stores one more unknown, the constant reference value @f$ K_0 @f$
of @f$ K @f$ which enters the slicing condition, i.e. it works with 59
unknowns in total.

Both formulations are now available in the code for different scenarios.
Users are referred to Appendix B of the paper below for more details on the
equations and formulations used in ExaGRyPE.


# A hands-on, unphysical explanation

When we simulate time-dependent partial differential equations, we typically
time-step through the solution.
To illustrate this, we could say that the solution itself exists in a
d+1-dimensional space (the +1 is time), and we slice through it every time
we take a snapshot.
The illustration below shows this for a d=2 setup:

@image html documentation/exagrype/flat-space-time.png width=400px

We start at time t=0 with a mesh (aka grid).
Each time step then performs another simulation step, possibly on a changed
grid, and yields a new snapshot, as illustrated by the coloured plane.
Effectively, we reduce the d+1-dimensional problem to a sequence of
d-dimensional problems through time marching.

Even with rudimentary A-level physics, we know that Einstein had the
breakthrough idea that space and time are distorted once we add mass to a
system.
In the extreme case of a black hole, spacetime experiences significant
distortions, which lead to a situation where not even light can escape from a
certain region around the black hole.
The boundary of this region is called the event horizon - if you move beyond it, no event gets out of region anymore.
There are many good, accessible pages providing further details, but for our
purposes the image below is sufficient to explain the next steps:

<center>
<img src="https://cdn.britannica.com/64/91964-050-4FDB21E8/vicinity-mass-distance-distortion-continuum-amount-relativity.jpg" width="400px" /> 
<br />
From <a href="https://www.britannica.com/science/relativity/Curved-space-time-and-geometric-gravitation">Britannica</a>.
</center>

This model of curved spacetime means that our original concept of time slices
breaks down:
The Einstein equations describing spacetime actually form a wave equation, and
instead of a time slice, we now end up with a so-called foliation of the
d+1-dimensional (infinitely large) domain.
This curved submanifold represents something that we could consider to be a
time step, even though the use of this term is itself somewhat flawed.

@image html documentation/exagrype/distorted-mesh.png width=400px

There are different ways to break this down into a simulation.
In ExaGRyPE, we follow the standard shift-lapse technique, which we can
illustrate as follows:
We still cut through the domain in a particular way, but then store a vector
per degree of freedom, e.g. per mesh point if we employ Finite Differences.
This vector tells us how spacetime at this reference point is distorted.
Thus, when we write a snapshot, this is no longer really a time step.
Different points might be at different positions in time, and they are also
displaced in space.
The whole mesh wobbles.

The relation between the individual snapshots and their sample points is now
given by a partial differential equation that describes the mesh.
We solve exactly this equation.
Unlike PDEs that we see in other fields, the PDE in the baseline ExaHyPE code
does not describe a quantity such as temperature sitting on the mesh, but the
deformation of the mesh itself.

<img src="https://arxiv.org/html/2406.11626v3/images/lapse_shift.png" width="400px" />

We can then place actual matter on top of this solution.
Obviously, any PDE we put on top may not use the standard flat-spacetime notion
of geometry.
Instead, it has to take into account that the underlying spacetime is
distorted.
In return, it feeds back into the spacetime PDE by injecting a right-hand-side
term.


# Building ExaGRyPE

ExaGRyPE sits on top of ExaHyPE 2 and hosts the C++ code that all ExaGRyPE
applications share.
ExaGRyPE is enabled like any other Peano extension or toolbox:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

or, with CMake,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DENABLE_EXAHYPE=ON -DENABLE_BLOCKSTRUCTURED=ON -DENABLE_LOADBALANCING=ON -DENABLE_EXAGRYPE=ON
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

As the Einstein equations are 3+1-dimensional and we use a foliation,
the library is built for 3d only, i.e. there is a \c libExaGRyPECore3d.a plus
its \c _debug, \c _asserts, \c _trace and \c _stats variants, but no 2d
counterpart.
If enabled, configure adds the library to the 3d link line of all generated
Makefiles.
Its unit tests run as part of \c UnitTests3d.


## Python API

The package \c exagrype.api (in \c src/exagrype/api) is layered on top of
\c exahype2.api.
It hosts the ExaGRyPE core solvers, such as
@ref exagrype.api.FOCCZ4Solver, which are in turn subclasses of
@ref exahype2.api.Solver.
These ExaGRyPE-specific solvers configure the base class with the correct
number of unknowns and PDEs (59 unknowns are used by FOCCZ4, for example), the
CCZ4 parameters, and all PDE terms of the particular system.
Users no longer have to add PDE terms themselves.
You still have to pick a well-suited numerical scheme, however:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver = exagrype.api.FOCCZ4Solver("CCZ4", initial_condition=exagrype.api.initialvalues.gauge_wave())
solver.numerical_scheme = exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithOptimisticExplicitEuler(solver, patch_size)
solver.grid_construction = exahype2.api.fv.SetupSimulationOverRegularGrid(solver, volume_size=0.04)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The example highlights that the module \c exagrype.api.initialvalues provides
some analytical initial conditions that users can pick from.
Obviously, there is still the opportunity to inject tailored initial
conditions, as with any other ExaHyPE solver.


## Black holes and GSL

Setting up proper initial conditions is not trivial.
ExaGRyPE relies on standard tools from the field to obtain physically
meaningful conditions.
The most popular initial-condition generator is likely TwoPunctures for binary
(and single) black holes.

TwoPunctures requires the GNU Scientific Library (GSL). It is therefore only
compiled into the library if you configure Peano with \c --with-gsl
(\c -DWITH_GSL=ON for CMake).
This defines \c UseGSL and adds GSL to the link line of all generated
Makefiles.

Applications switch black-hole initial data on per build through
\c -DIncludeTwoPunctures.
If an application sets this flag but Peano has been configured without GSL,
the build stops with an error message telling you to reconfigure.


